From f85c89578fa7e6fa04d76531fea7051164857cae
Subject: web-worker: run the render world on its own web worker
Origin: https://github.com/robtfm/bevy/commit/f85c89578fa7e6fa04d76531fea7051164857cae
Date: 2026-09-18T23:39:16+01:00

With the `web-worker` feature, DefaultPlugins now adds
PipelinedRenderingPlugin on wasm, and the render loop runs as a spawn_local
task on whichever worker calls `cleanup` (the one owning the wgpu device and
the OffscreenCanvas). The app worker blocks on the handoff channel with
`block_on`, since the single-threaded task pool cannot wait cross-thread.

Supporting pieces:
- `bevy_render::web_worker`: the render worker's side. `render_worker_main`
  creates the wgpu instance, device and a surface on the transferred
  canvas (unless the app `provide_render_resources` itself), then hosts
  the app for `finish`/`cleanup` and hands it back. WebGPU handles are
  bound to the worker that created them, so the device must be created,
  and every plugin's device-touching setup must run, there.
- `RenderCreation::WebWorker`: the render plugin takes the resources from
  the worker's slot in `finish`, and installs `init_on_render_worker` as
  the app's finisher so `App::run` ships the app across for
  `finish`/`cleanup` before the runner starts.
- `App::set_finisher`: a hook `App::run` passes the app through before the
  runner, for running `finish`/`cleanup` elsewhere. The winit runner already
  skips them for an app that arrives cleaned.
- `PrecreatedSurface` (crate-internal): the primary window's surface comes
  from the render worker instead of being created from the winit raw
  handle, which lives on the app worker.
- `render_system` drops the swap-chain view right after present, so
  `extract_windows` never drops a wgpu handle on the app thread; its own
  drop of the view is removed as it is always `None` by then.
- `no_automatic_skin_batching` tolerates a main world without a RenderDevice
  (interim; a main-world limits resource replaces this).
---
